@database "decision"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: decision.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Decision"
@{h1}Decision

This plugin lets an agent ask a decision model -- TypeSafe's Jev, or a
local Laya, whichever the decision profile names -- questions about many
pieces of content at once. A decision model writes no text: for each item
it answers each question with a number, a probability for a yes/no question
or a position on an ordered scale for a rating. One tool call takes up to
250 items and 20 questions; the plugin sends one request per item, ten at
a time, and gives back a compact table of numbers. That is faster and far
cheaper than having a chat model judge the same items one by one. It has no
hooks and no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Probabilities  " link probabilities}
  @{"  Scores  " link scores}
  @{"  Answers and errors  " link answers}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry shipped in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    decision:
      type: decision
      enabled: true
@{body}

No agent has the tools by default. Allow them in an agent's list:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+decision/*"
@{body}

@{h2}Which model judges

The plugin names no model itself. It asks for a client by decision
profile: @{tt}decision_profile@{utt} in its entry, or, when that is empty,
@{tt}llm_system.default_decision_profile@{utt} (shipped: @{tt}jev@{utt},
Jev through OpenRouter). Profiles and models live under
@{tt}llm_system.decision_profiles@{utt} and
@{tt}llm_system.decision_models@{utt}, beside the chat models (see
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}). The shipped
configuration also has a profile @{tt}laya@{utt} for a local laya-serve.
Different models give different numbers for the same question: a threshold
tuned on Jev does not carry over to Laya.

The client is built at the first call and kept until the plugin is
restarted. A profile that does not exist, or no default at all, makes every
call answer "Failed to initialize decision client: ..." with the reason.

Timeout and retries belong to the decision model's entry, not to this
plugin: @{tt}request_timeout@{utt} (60 seconds) and @{tt}max_retries@{utt}
(2, so three attempts, waiting 2 and 4 seconds, or the host's Retry-After up
to 60 s -- asked for more, the item fails at once) apply per item; see
@{"llm_decisions" link "llm_decisions/retries"}.

@{h2}Server settings

They go straight into the entry, beside @{tt}type@{utt} -- not in a
@{tt}config:@{utt} block, which the plugin does not read. A value that is
not a number stops the plugin from loading.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
decision_profile | (empty) | the decision profile; empty: llm_system.default_decision_profile
max_batch_size | 250 | items per call at most; at least 1
max_concurrency | 10 | requests running at the same time at most; a call may ask for fewer, never more; at least 1
max_questions | 20 | questions (or criteria) per call at most; at least 1
max_context_length | 50000 | characters per item at most; an object or array counts as its JSON text; at least 1
default_scale | ["1", "2", "3", "4", "5"] | the scale of a criterion that names none; a list of fewer than two levels is ignored
@{body}

@{h2}Dependencies and data

None beyond the core; the client comes from the @{tt}llm_decisions@{utt}
plugin, which must be present. The key is the one of the endpoint's host
(OpenRouter's key for OpenRouter). The plugin stores nothing.
@endnode

@node probabilities "Probabilities"
@{h1}Probabilities

@{tt}decision_evaluate_probabilities@{utt} (the name follows the plugin
instance) asks yes/no questions about every item and answers with a
probability from 0 to 1 per item and question: how likely the answer is
yes.

@{h2}Parameters

The framework does not check the arguments against the schema; what
follows is what the code does with them.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
items | required; a list of 1 to max_batch_size items. An item is an object with @{tt}context@{utt} and an optional @{tt}id@{utt}, or plain text
items[].id | the item's key in the answer; missing or empty: @{tt}item_1@{utt}, @{tt}item_2@{utt}, ... by position. Two items with the same id: an error
items[].context | required; the content to judge. Leading and trailing spaces are removed; empty is an error. An object or array is also taken and sent as it is
questions | required; a list of 1 to max_questions questions, each an object or plain text
questions[].id | the question's key in the answer; missing or empty: @{tt}q1@{utt}, @{tt}q2@{utt}, ... by position. Two questions with the same id: an error
questions[].question | required; what to decide, e.g. "Is this command safe to run?"
questions[].criteria_true, criteria_false | what makes the answer yes, and what makes it no; both or neither, one alone is an error
max_concurrency | how many items are sent at the same time; default and upper bound the server's max_concurrency, below 1 is 1, not a number is ignored
include_details | true adds @{tt}details@{utt} to the answer (see below); default false
@{body}

All questions go to the model together, once per item: the price of a call
grows with the number of questions more than with the length of the item.

@{h2}The details

With @{tt}include_details@{utt}, @{tt}details.<item>.<question>@{utt}
holds @{tt}value@{utt} (the probability again), @{tt}type@{utt}
(@{tt}noul@{utt}, the model's name for a yes/no question),
@{tt}confidence@{utt} and @{tt}probabilities@{utt}. Jev sends the
probability alone, so the last two are usually null.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"items": [{"id": "a", "context": "git status"},
           {"id": "b", "context": "rm -rf /tmp/build"}],
 "questions": [{"id": "safe", "question": "Is this command safe to run?"}]}
@{body}

@{code json}
{"status": "success",
 "results": {"a": {"safe": 0.98}, "b": {"safe": 0.33}},
 "summary": {"total_items": 2, "successful_items": 2, "failed_items": 0,
             "total_cost": 3e-05, "total_input_tokens": 690,
             "total_output_tokens": 2, "model": "typesafe/jev-1.13-20260917"}}
@{body}
@endnode

@node scores "Scores"
@{h1}Scores

@{tt}decision_evaluate_scores@{utt} rates every item on one or more
ordered scales. The answer per item and criterion is a POSITION on the
scale, counted from 0: on @{tt}["none", "low", "high"]@{utt}, 0 is none, 2
is high, and 0.58 lies between none and low. It is a point, not a rung --
2.96 on a four-level scale is a real answer. On the default scale
@{tt}["1", "2", "3", "4", "5"]@{utt} the numbers run from 0 to 4: a score
of 3 means the label "4".

@{h2}Parameters

@{tt}items@{utt}, @{tt}max_concurrency@{utt} and @{tt}include_details@{utt}
work as for @{" probabilities " link probabilities}.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
criteria | required; a list of 1 to max_questions criteria, each an object (plain text is an error here)
criteria[].id | the criterion's key in the answer; missing or empty: @{tt}c1@{utt}, @{tt}c2@{utt}, ... by position. Two with the same id: an error
criteria[].question | required; what to rate. @{tt}instruction@{utt} is taken in its place
criteria[].scale | the levels, lowest first, at least two; numbers are turned into text. Missing: the server's default_scale
@{body}

@{h2}The details

With @{tt}include_details@{utt}, @{tt}details.<item>.<criterion>@{utt}
holds @{tt}score@{utt}, @{tt}scale@{utt} (the levels used),
@{tt}confidence@{utt}, @{tt}probabilities@{utt} -- keyed by position as
text, @{tt}{"0": 0.01, "2": 0.97}@{utt} -- and @{tt}legend@{utt}, which
maps the positions back to the labels.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"items": ["rm -rf /tmp/build"],
 "criteria": [{"id": "risk", "question": "How risky is this command?",
               "scale": ["none", "low", "high"]}]}
@{body}

@{code json}
{"status": "success",
 "results": {"item_1": {"risk": 1.1}},
 "summary": {"total_items": 1, "successful_items": 1, "failed_items": 0,
             "total_cost": 1.6e-05, "total_input_tokens": 410,
             "total_output_tokens": 1, "model": "typesafe/jev-1.13-20260917"},
 "scores": {"risk": 1.1}}
@{body}
@endnode

@node answers "Answers and errors"
@{h1}Answers and errors

@{h2}The answer

@{table}
Field | Meaning
status | success; partial_success when some items failed; error
results | per item id, per question or criterion id, the number
errors | per failed item id, why it failed; only when some did
details | only with include_details
summary | total_items, successful_items, failed_items, total_cost, total_input_tokens, total_output_tokens, model
probabilities / scores | a call with exactly one item that succeeded also gets that item's numbers here, a copy of its entry in results
@{body}

@{tt}model@{utt} is the model that answered the first successful item, as
the endpoint names it (an alias such as @{tt}~typesafe/jev-latest@{utt}
comes back with its dated version). The token and cost totals count every
call that was answered, including one whose answer was refused (a question
left unanswered), since it was billed. @{tt}total_cost@{utt} is null when
any of them came without a price -- a local Laya and TypeSafe's own
endpoint report none; the sum of the others would read as the whole.

@{h2}Failed items

Every item is its own request. One that fails -- the endpoint refused it,
could not be reached after its retries, or left a question unanswered --
lands in @{tt}errors@{utt}, and the rest go on. Only when every item
failed is the status @{tt}error@{utt}: @{tt}error@{utt} says "All <n>
items failed evaluation: <the first reason>", @{tt}errors@{utt} lists
them all, and @{tt}summary@{utt} carries what the failed calls cost.

@{h2}Refused before anything is sent

These come back as @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": ...}@{utt}, and no
request is made:

@{table}
error | When
Items must be a non-empty list of context items. | items missing, empty or not a list
Batch size (<n>) exceeds maximum allowed (<max>). | more than max_batch_size items
Item at index <i> must be a string or object. | an item is a number, a list, ...
Duplicate item ID '<id>' at index <i>. | two items with one id
Item '<id>' context validation error: <reason> | the context is missing ("Context is required and must not be null."), empty ("Context must not be empty or whitespace only."), too long ("Context length (<n>) exceeds maximum allowed (<max>).") or a number
Questions must be a non-empty list. / Criteria must be a non-empty list. | questions or criteria missing or empty
Question count (<n>) exceeds limit (<max>). / Criteria count ... | more than max_questions
Question at index <i> must be a string or object. | a question is a number, a list, ...
Question at index <i> has empty text. | no question text
Question at index <i> gives only one of criteria_true and criteria_false; give both or neither. | one criteria side alone
Criterion at index <i> must be an object. | a criterion given as plain text
Criterion at index <i> has empty question/instruction. | no text to rate by
Criterion '<id>' scale must be an ordered list of at least 2 levels. | a scale that is not a list, has one level, or holds objects
Duplicate question ID '<id>' at index <i>. / Duplicate criterion ID ... | two with one id
Failed to initialize decision client: <reason> | no usable decision profile (see @{" Setting it up " link setup})
@{body}

@{h2}Cancelling

Stopping the run stops the batch: items not yet sent are not sent, those
in flight are dropped, and the tool call ends as cancelled with no answer.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

Two tools, @{tt}<instance>_evaluate_probabilities@{utt} and
@{tt}<instance>_evaluate_scores@{utt}. Their descriptions and parameters
come from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}; the scores tool tells the
model that a score is a position counted from 0. The schema lists
@{tt}items@{utt} and @{tt}questions@{utt} as objects; plain text is taken
as well.

@{h2}The chat status line

While a call runs: "Evaluating <n> items against <m> questions" (or
"scores"), then "Evaluated <k>/<n> items" about every fifth of the batch.
At the end: "<n> items evaluated (<m> questions each)", or "<k>/<n> items
evaluated (<f> failed)"; a refusal shows the error text, cut to 140
characters.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The answer is an ordinary tool result, appended to the conversation;
nothing earlier is rewritten, so the prompt cache stays intact. It holds
numbers and ids only, one number per item and question, plus the
summary. @{tt}include_details@{utt} multiplies that, and a single-item
call carries its numbers twice (@{tt}results@{utt} and
@{tt}probabilities@{utt} or @{tt}scores@{utt}). The decision calls
themselves go to the decision model, not the chat model, and are booked
under their own provider (@{tt}openrouter_decisions@{utt},
@{tt}systemone_decisions@{utt}) in the usage tracker and the message
debugger.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Only yes/no and scale questions. The model's third type, a choice
between named options, has no tool here.
@{bullet}Jev is not deterministic: the same question on the same item can
come back a few hundredths apart.
@{bullet}Without @{tt}include_details@{utt} the answer does not repeat the
scale, so the agent has to remember which positions mean what.
@endnode
